   "original_caption": "(Figure 19.14) The complexes (a) [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺, (b) [Cu(Cl)₄]²⁻, and (c) [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ have coordination numbers of two, four, and six, respectively. The geometries of these complexes are the same as we have seen with VSEPR theory for main group elements: linear, tetrahedral, and octahedral. (Figure 19.18) These are geometries of some complexes with coordination numbers of seven and eight. (Figure 19.19) Many transition metal complexes adopt octahedral geometries, with six donor atoms forming bond angles of 90° about the central atom with adjacent ligands. Note that only ligands within the coordination sphere affect the geometry around the metal center. (Figure 19.20) Transition metals with a coordination number of four can adopt a tetrahedral geometry (a) as in K₂[Zn(CN)₄] or a square planar geometry (b) as shown in [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂].",
